{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Stop `rebar init` from silently creating an empty divergent store when it cannot obtain\n`origin/tickets`. This is the real fix for open P1 **01e8-302b** (bilingual-capitalist-caecilian).\nsession_log **1cb6-d553-6565-49b4** (measured facts; do not re-derive).\n\n## Why a faster fetch is not the fix\nAt `init.py:366-382` the fetch at `:375` runs **only if `origin/tickets` already resolves**. If the\nouter clone's fetch timed out so the remote-tracking ref never materialized, both `local` and\n`remote` are falsy and control falls through to `git worktree add --orphan` at `:381` — silently\nbootstrapping a fresh empty store. Consequence, as reported: `rebar list` returns `[]`, all existing\nwork is invisible, and new writes land on a branch that cannot fast-forward onto `origin/tickets`.\nA faster fetch lowers the probability; it does not remove the failure mode, and a slow agent git\nproxy will still time out sometimes.\n\n## Second affected site (plan-review G1G2/G6 remediation)\nThe same predicate class appears in **`pending_init_attaches_to_existing` (`init.py:628`)**, which\ndecides whether an init merely ATTACHES to an existing store (and may therefore proceed\nnon-interactively) by asking whether a `tickets` branch exists locally or on `origin`. When the\nremote-tracking ref never materialized because the fetch timed out, that predicate is **also**\nfalsely negative — so init both loses its attach-detection AND falls through to the orphan path.\nFixing only `_mount_or_create_branch` would leave the auto-init gate mis-classifying the same\nfailure. Both sites must consult the same remote-advertisement probe.\n\n## Approach\nBefore orphan-bootstrapping, probe the configured remote. If it is reachable **and advertises the\nconfigured tickets branch**, refuse to bootstrap: exit non-zero with a message naming the remote, the\nbranch, and the remediation. Only bootstrap when the remote genuinely has no such branch (the\nlegitimate first-init case). Keep the probe bounded so an unreachable remote does not hang init.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] With a reachable remote that **advertises** `tickets`, a simulated fetch failure causes a\n      **non-zero exit** and **no orphan branch is created** (`git branch --list tickets` empty). Tier:\n      **CLI subprocess** against a real temporary remote. This is the RED case for P1 01e8-302b.\n- [ ] With a remote that has **no** `tickets` branch, init still bootstraps successfully — the\n      negative control that keeps first-init working. Tier: **CLI subprocess**.\n- [ ] With an **unreachable** remote, init fails within a bounded time rather than hanging. Tier:\n      **CLI subprocess** against a blackholed endpoint.\n- [ ] The error message names the remote, the branch, and a concrete remediation. Tier: **unit** on\n      the message.\n- [ ] An existing healthy store is unaffected (idempotent re-init). Tier: **CLI subprocess**.\n      Proving command: `pytest tests/integration/test_init_fail_closed.py -k idempotent -v`.\n- [ ] `pending_init_attaches_to_existing` returns **True** when the remote advertises `tickets` even\n      though the local remote-tracking ref is absent (the false-negative this shares with\n      `_mount_or_create_branch`). Tier: **unit** with a real temporary remote.\n      Proving command: `pytest tests/unit/test_init_attach_predicate.py -v`.\n- [ ] Both call sites resolve remote existence through **one** shared helper, asserted by a test that\n      would fail if a second independent predicate were reintroduced. Tier: **unit**.\n\n## Files\n`src/rebar/_commands/init.py`, tests\n\nScope: S-M. No dependencies."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785379551020851001,"uuid":"c8ec0660-44e1-4e9f-8d9b-176e279eba8b"}